WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.800
How do you know the sensor is healthy?

2
00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.425
On the device, the
sense service is running

3
00:00:04.425 --> 00:00:07.350
and the onboarding state
in the registry is one.

4
00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:11.031
In the service, the device info
table carries two columns:

5
00:00:11.031 --> 00:00:13.850
onboarding status, and
sensor health state.

6
00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:19.113
Microsoft Learn's device inventory field
reference lists five sensor health values.

7
00:00:19.113 --> 00:00:20.563
Active is the pass.

8
00:00:20.563 --> 00:00:24.787
Inactive means no contact,
typically for seven days or more.

9
00:00:24.787 --> 00:00:29.150
Then no sensor data, impaired
communications, and misconfigured.

10
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:33.164
Every value but active is a ticket;
for impaired communications,

11
00:00:33.164 --> 00:00:36.950
start with the government U R L
list the firewall has to allow.

12
00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:42.362
Intune's E D R onboarding status report
shows the same thing from the Intune side.

13
00:00:42.362 --> 00:00:46.446
The guide sets the day-two rule: the
operator reviews coverage weekly,

14
00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:50.674
and every device that hasn't been
active for seven days becomes a ticket.

15
00:00:50.674 --> 00:00:53.487
The weekly coverage file
is kept twelve months.

16
00:00:53.487 --> 00:00:57.400
The platform owner reviews the
exception register every quarter.

17
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.925
And the assessor-ready
artifact is coverage by ring,

18
00:01:00.925 --> 00:01:04.510
against the inventory the
C M eight control asks for.
